    written: [
      W(1, "What is a startup?", "A startup is a newly started, often innovative business that aims to grow.", "short"),
      W(1, "Which Jātaka tale is mentioned in the chapter, and what does it show?", "The tale of the dead mouse; it shows how initiative, small steps and hard work can build wealth.", "short"),
      W(2, "Differentiate between revenue and expenses.", "Revenue is the money earned from sales.\nExpenses are the costs of running the business.", "short"),
      W(2, "A shop has revenue of ₹40,000 and total costs of ₹33,500. Find its profit.", "Profit = revenue − costs = 40,000 − 33,500 = ₹6,500.", "short"),
      W(2, "Why can failures be useful to an entrepreneur?", "Failures and risks are part of the journey; they teach lessons about customers, costs and mistakes that can be used to improve the next attempt.", "short"),
      W(3, "How does the story of Mohan Singh Oberoi show entrepreneurship?", "1. He was born in 1898 in Punjab and started as a hotel clerk in Shimla.\n2. He bought his first hotel in 1934, taking a risk.\n3. He built a major hotel chain, creating value and jobs.", "short"),
      W(3, "Explain with an example why a business can face trouble even when it earns a profit on paper.", "1. Cash flow is the movement of money in and out.\n2. Example: a shop sells on credit, so profit is shown, but customers pay late.\n3. The shop cannot pay suppliers on time, so it must watch its cash flow to stay sustainable.", "short"),
      W(4, "Explain how the four functional areas would be used in running a school canteen.", "1. Management and operations: planning the menu, preparing food and checking quality.\n2. Finance and accounting: budgeting, keeping cash records and checking cash flow.\n3. Human resources: hiring and managing the cooks and helpers.\n4. Marketing and sales: telling students about items and offers, and selling them.", "long"),
      W(5, "How do startups contribute to the economy, society and the environment? Support with the Lijjat Papad example.", "1. Startups drive innovation.\n2. They create jobs and solve real problems.\n3. They support the vision of Viksit Bharat 2047.\n4. Lijjat Papad began in 1959 with seven women and ₹80 and grew into a large women-owned enterprise, giving livelihoods.\n5. Responsible entrepreneurs also think about their impact on society and the environment.", "long"),
      W(4, "Case study: Gurpreet runs a small bakery. In a day she sells 500 buns at ₹12 each. She spends ₹2,200 on flour, ₹600 on gas and ₹1,000 on a helper.\n(i) Find her revenue.\n(ii) Find her total costs.\n(iii) Find her profit and name one way to increase it.", "(i) Revenue = 500 × 12 = ₹6,000.\n(ii) Costs = 2,200 + 600 + 1,000 = ₹3,800.\n(iii) Profit = 6,000 − 3,800 = ₹2,200. She can increase it by raising sales through better marketing or by cutting costs.", "case")
    ]
